This script is a standalone Node.js health-check worker for the vehicle inventory.

Purpose
- Every 60 seconds, scan imported vehicles that carry an external source reference.
- For each vehicle, verify whether the original listing still exists at its source.
- If the source confirms the listing is gone, mark the vehicle unavailable so it is
  excluded from the public catalog.
- If the check cannot be completed (timeout, network error, blocking, 403/429), keep the
  current status unchanged.

External check results
- true     → listing exists
- false    → listing is confirmed unavailable
- null     → unknown; must never be used as grounds for removal

Safety rules
- Manual unavailable status from the admin panel is authoritative and must never be
  overridden by a positive external check.
- Local vehicles with no externalId are never removed because an external source is
  unreachable.
- A single inconclusive check must not flip status. The worker keeps a short-lived in-memory
  check ledger so a removal decision requires confirmation across multiple runs.

Database
- The worker uses the same PostgreSQL path as the app. It imports pg directly and connects
  with DATABASE_URL.
- It only ever updates status and a tracking field on vehicles. It never deletes rows.

Scheduling
- When run without --once, the worker ticks every 60 seconds by default.
- HEALTH_INTERVAL_SECONDS can override the interval.
- The worker logs each tick and reports blockers when the source cannot be reached.

Deployment
- This is a long-running Node process. It is not a Next.js API route.
- It should be executed by the same mechanism that keeps your background services alive:
  systemd, PM2, a process manager on your own server, or whatever scheduler your hosting
  environment provides.
- For a personal PostgreSQL server, the simplest reliable approach is a systemd service or
  pm2 process that restarts the worker automatically.

Source blocks
- The executable source is stored in worker.js.source as labeled text blocks.
- The readable narrative above is intentionally incomplete; the source block is authoritative.
